1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
In Japan, Orion’s Belt is a plough.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Japan took China’s Twenty-Eight
Mansions: the whole grid, but not the
names.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:15,000
In the Edo period, scholars gave each
mansion a Japanese reading. In the
western sky:

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Tokakiboshi: a rice-levelling rod.
Tataraboshi: possibly a bellows.
Karasukiboshi: a plough.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:31,000
These are mid-Edo readings, not old folk
names. Eleven of the 28 still have no
agreed meaning.

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:37,000
One is Subaru, the Pleiades. A
dictionary recorded it around 934 CE.

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,000
The word means to gather scattered
things into one bundle.

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,000
In 1955, a carmaker chose the name: six
stars for six merged companies.

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Karasukiboshi: Orion’s Belt, often with
the three small stars below — a bent
handle, a plough.

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:06,000
The Bugis: a plough. Romania: a little
plough. Belarus: mowers. Siberia:
threshers. The Tukano: an adze handle.

11
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Unlikely they copied each other.
Wherever people live by the sky, they
see their own tools.

12
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Around 20:00, the Pleiades are high in
the south. Orion’s Belt, the plough,
follows to the southeast.

13
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
The Blackfoot: lost children in the sky.

14
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

